.Dd October 22, 2026
.Dt ELF_SET_LOAD_HEADERS 3
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm elf_set_load_headers
.Nd whether an executable's headers are loaded with its code
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libelfobj
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In elfobj.h
.Ft elf_err_t
.Fn elf_set_load_headers "elfobj_t *obj" "int load"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
When an executable or shared object is written, its first executable
.Dv PT_LOAD
segment takes in the ELF header and the program headers where they fit
below the segment's first address, so that the headers are in memory
with the program.
.Fn elf_set_load_headers
with
.Fa load
0 leaves them out: the segment begins with its first section.
A linker script's
.Ic PHDRS
command without
.Ic FILEHDR
says so; the i386 Linux kernel's text segment begins with its code, at the
physical address its decompressor puts it at.
.Pp
With
.Fa load
non-zero, the default, they are taken in again.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn elf_set_load_headers
returns
.Dv ELF_OK ,
or
.Dv ELF_ERR_STATE
for a
.Dv NULL
object or one opened read-only or finalized.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Write an image whose segments hold only its sections.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <elfobj.h>

elf_err_t
write_bare(elfobj_t *obj, const char *path)
{
    elf_err_t err = elf_set_load_headers(obj, 0);

    return err != ELF_OK ? err : elf_write_file(obj, path);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ld 1 ,
.Xr elf_section_lma 3
